<exercises xml:id="s_matrix_algebra_ex">
<subexercises>
 
<exercise>
  <statement>
    <p>
      In this exercise you will complete the proof of <xref ref="th_matrix_alg_props"/>.
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <p>
          Prove the <xref ref="th_matrix_leftdist" text="title"/>.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Prove the <xref ref="th_matrix_rightdist" text="title"/>.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Prove the <xref ref="th_matrixscale_comm" text="title"/>.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </statement>

</exercise>
<exercise>
  <statement>
    <p>
      Prove <xref ref="th_matrix_add_mult_ident"/>.
    </p>
  </statement>

</exercise>
<exercise>
  <statement>
    <p>
      In this exercise you will complete the proof of <xref ref="th_trans_props"/>.
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <p>
          Prove <xref ref="th_trans_scale">Statement</xref>.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Prove <xref ref="th_trans_prod">Statement</xref>.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Prove <xref ref="th_trans_owninverse">Statement</xref>.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
  <statement>
    <p>
      Let <m>A</m> an <m>n\times n</m> matrix.
      We define its <term>square</term> <m>A^2</m>
      as <m>A^2=AA</m>.
    </p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            In real number algebra we know that <m>a^2=0\implies a=0</m>. By contrast, show
            that there are infinitely many <m>2\times 2</m> matrices <m>A</m> satisfying <m>A^2=\boldzero_{2\times 2}</m>.
          </p>
          <p>
            Optional: can you describe in a parametric manner the set of <em>all</em> matrices <m>A</m> satisfying <m>A^2=\boldzero_{2\times 2}</m>?
          </p>

        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            In real number algebra we know that <m>a^2=a\implies a=0 \text{ or } a=1</m>. By contrast, show
            that there are infinitely many <m>2\times 2</m> matrices <m>A</m> satisfying <m>A^2=A</m>.
          </p>

        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            In real number algebra we have the identity <m>(x+y)^2=x^2+2xy+y^2</m>. Show that two <m>n\times n</m> matrices <m>A</m>, <m>B</m> satisfy
            <md>
              (A+B)^2=A^2+2AB+B^2
            </md>
            if and only if <m>AB=BA</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
  </statement>
  <hint>
    <p>
       For (a) set <m>A=\abcdmatrix{a}{b}{c}{d}</m>, compute <m>A^2</m>, set this matrix equal to <m>\boldzero_{2\times 2}</m>, and try and find some solutions to the corresponding (nonlinear) system of four equations in the unknowns <m>a,b,c,d</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Similar hint for (b), only now set <m>A^2=A</m>.
    </p>
  </hint>
</exercise>
<exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>
      Consider the matrix equation
      <m>A\, \begin{bmatrix}
        1\amp 1\\ 1\amp 1
      \end{bmatrix}=\begin{bmatrix}
        1\amp 1\\ 1\amp 1
      \end{bmatrix}</m>.
    </p>
    </introduction>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
     The following chain of implications is invalid.
      <md>
        <mrow> A\, \begin{bmatrix}
          1\amp 1\\ 1\amp 1
        \end{bmatrix}=\begin{bmatrix}
          1\amp 1\\ 1\amp 1
        \end{bmatrix}\amp\implies A\, \begin{bmatrix}
          1\amp 1\\ 1\amp 1
        \end{bmatrix}=I_2\, \begin{bmatrix}
          1\amp 1\\ 1\amp 1
        \end{bmatrix} </mrow>
        <mrow> \amp\implies A=I_2 </mrow>
      </md>.
    For each implication in the chain, explain why it is valid or invalid.
    </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
      Find all <m>A</m> satisfying <md>A\, \begin{bmatrix}
        1\amp 1\\ 1\amp 1
      \end{bmatrix}=\begin{bmatrix}
        1\amp 1\\ 1\amp 1
      \end{bmatrix}</md>.
      </p>
      </statement>
      <hint>
        <p>
          Write <m>A=\begin{bmatrix}
            a\amp b\\ c\amp d
          \end{bmatrix}</m> and set up a system of linear equations in the unknowns <m>a,b,c,d</m>.
        </p>
      </hint>
    </task>
</exercise>
</subexercises>
</exercises>
